<script>
import DealNote from 'crm/src/pipeline/lib/DealNote.abide'
import dealNoteSource from 'crm/src/pipeline/lib/DealNote.abide' with { type: 'text' }
import dealNoteTestSource from 'crm/src/pipeline/lib/DealNote.test.ts' with { type: 'text' }
import saveNoteSource from 'crm/src/pipeline/saveNote.handler.ts' with { type: 'text' }

import Doc from '#src/lib/Doc.abide'
import Example from '#src/lib/Example.abide'
import Registry from '#src/lib/Registry.abide'
import Section from '#src/lib/Section.abide'
</script>

<Doc>
    <Section title="`watch` runs something outside the page when a value changes">
        <Example
            id="autosave"
            app="crm"
            title="`watch(effect)` — save a note a beat after the typing stops"
            summary="A note on a deal, saved to the server while you type. Type a sentence quickly, and watch the saved line."
            files={{
                'src/pipeline/lib/DealNote.abide': dealNoteSource,
                'src/pipeline/saveNote.handler.ts': saveNoteSource,
                'src/pipeline/lib/DealNote.test.ts': dealNoteTestSource,
            }}
        >
            <DealNote />
        </Example>

        <p><code>watch</code> runs its effect now, and again whenever a value it read changes.
            The effect returns a <code>Disposer</code>, which runs before the next run and when the
            component goes. Each keystroke clears the timer the one before it started, so a burst of
            typing is one save.</p>

        <p>The draft is read in the effect, not inside the timer: a read there happens after
            tracking ends and subscribes to nothing. <code>saved.peek()</code> reads the saved note
            without subscribing, so saving it does not wake the watch again.</p>

        <table>
            <thead><tr><th>Name</th><th>What it does for the pipeline</th></tr></thead>
            <tbody>
                <tr><td><code>watch</code></td><td><code>watch(effect)</code> wakes on whatever the effect read; <code>watch(sources, effect)</code> wakes only on the values listed</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>s.watch</code></td><td><code>watch</code> over one value, handed to the effect</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>Effect</code></td><td>the function a watch runs, returning nothing or a <code>Disposer</code></td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>Disposer</code></td><td>what tears the previous run down</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>s.peek</code></td><td>reads what is held without subscribing</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>flushSync</code></td><td>runs every effect a write woke before it returns, for a handler that measures or scrolls the page the write left</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>isServer</code></td><td>whether the effect is running on the server, for work only a browser can do</td></tr>
            </tbody>
        </table>

        <p>A watch runs on the server too, while the page renders. Guard work that needs the
            browser, such as <code>localStorage</code>, with <code>isServer()</code> rather than
            <code>typeof window</code>: a test renders the server half beside a DOM, and
            <code>isServer()</code> still answers for the side the work is on.</p>

        <p>The effect is the one place in this card where a read subscribes. Its
            <code>Disposer</code> does not, and nor does any function abide calls without rebuilding
            it:</p>

        <table>
            <thead><tr><th>A read in</th><th>Subscribes</th></tr></thead>
            <tbody>
                <tr><td><code>watch</code> effect, bare</td><td>to everything it reads</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>watch</code> effect, over sources</td><td>to the sources alone</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>Transformer</code>, <code>Disposer</code>, middleware, lifecycle hooks</td><td>to nothing: none of them is rebuilt</td></tr>
            </tbody>
        </table>
    </Section>

    <Registry />
</Doc>
